## Residual behavior and review notes

- Cleanup deliberately uses an ownership heuristic: it removes every byte-exact local legacy value and leaves all variants. A user who intentionally configured the exact local value will see it removed; documentation states this boundary.
- System, global, and included canonical values may remain effective because init does not mutate inherited policy. Documentation and tests make that scope explicit.
- Dry-run reports a read-only snapshot. A concurrent Git-config change after the preview can make a later apply differ.
- Fetch cleanup runs before push cleanup and mirror additions, so a later apply failure cannot leave the known hazardous direct fetch mapping in place.
- The parent still owns independent review, final gate disposition, capture/integration, tracker synchronization, commit, and any push.
- Parent process follow-up: the fresh scaffold references missing `scripts/seat-adapter.sh`; doctor reports two unrelated reviewer executable failures. Recorded in lane-status.md; no out-of-scope scaffold/process fix was attempted.

